1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Millions of tons of concrete hold back the force of a river.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Generators spin out billions of kilowatt hours to power cities.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Potable water flows for hundreds of miles.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Irrigation provides food for entire nations.

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:20,000
The world's largest structures restrain the fury of nature's floods

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
of what happens when disaster strikes.

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
It's engineering at its most massive scale

8
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,640
with both benefit and cost to the environment.

9
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:33,080
Now, Dams on Modern Marbles.

10
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:45,920
Water.

11
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:49,260
It's a giver and taker of life.

12
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:54,020
With it, barren land can be cultivated.

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Without it, no life can be sustained.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Because human history is replete with cycles of drought and flood,

15
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:12,000
we've sought both to conserve and to protect ourselves

16
00:01:12,001 --> 00:01:16,000
from this destructive yet essential resource.

17
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:20,000
If you're going to develop the resources of a nation,

18
00:01:20,001 --> 00:01:25,000
you want to not let the river just flow into the sea

19
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:27,000
without making some use of it.

20
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:32,760
And we maximize our water use with dams.

21
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:40,580
There are an estimated 900,000 dams worldwide.

22
00:01:40,581 --> 00:01:42,220
Most are small to medium sized.

23
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:48,000
But 40,000 are over 50 feet high.

24
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:54,660
The most familiar dams, the mega dams,

25
00:01:54,661 --> 00:02:01,620
A select club of about 300 over 450 feet high are vital pieces of civilization's infrastructure.

26
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:08,340
In the 1930s, we in the U.S. realized that we could manage entire river basins,

27
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:11,460
not just one single dam at a time, but dozens.

28
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:16,980
So we began developing river basins on a vast scale.

29
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:20,620
It is scale that makes dams unique.

30
00:02:20,621 --> 00:02:26,100
In the 1930s, dams in the United States became the largest constructions on Earth,

31
00:02:26,101 --> 00:02:28,920
and more than just huge feats of engineering.

32
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:34,200
They were seen as monuments, as symbols of progress.

33
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:40,820
We're using more material than has ever been used before since the time of the pyramids,

34
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:42,820
and that's something that you celebrate.

35
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:49,120
Grand Coulee Dam is the largest concrete structure in the United States.

36
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:55,200
Other giants include Hoover Dam and Oroville Dam.

37
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:57,620
But they are not new.

38
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:03,260
And because of environmental opposition and the lack of new sites on America's waterways,

39
00:03:04,260 --> 00:03:07,380
no dams this size will be built again in the United States.

40
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:13,420
But when the U.S. was building big dams, the developing world was taking note

41
00:03:13,421 --> 00:03:16,700
and with increasing regularity has followed suit.

42
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:19,420
Aswan Dam in Egypt.

43
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:25,620
Itaipu dam between Brazil and Paraguay.

44
00:03:25,621 --> 00:03:32,620
And finally, the most ambitious hydroelectric dam in the world, China's Three Gorges Dam,

45
00:03:32,621 --> 00:03:38,620
which will exceed all other dams in size and power production.

46
00:03:38,621 --> 00:03:44,620
Like any hydroelectric facility, Three Gorges Dam will harness the power of water released from an elevated reservoir

47
00:03:44,621 --> 00:03:47,620
to drive turbines and produce electricity.

48
00:03:47,621 --> 00:03:52,740
This process is standard. What's unusual is how much electricity it will produce.

49
00:03:53,500 --> 00:04:00,380
The hydroelectric generators that are attached to this dam will generate 18 million kilowatts of power,

50
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:04,960
an enormous amount of power, the equivalent of 18 nuclear plants.

51
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:12,860
That's enough electricity to light up Shanghai, China's most populous city of almost 10 million people.

52
00:04:12,861 --> 00:04:21,440
Construction on the Three Gorges Dam began in 1994 with the digging of a canal to reroute

53
00:04:21,441 --> 00:04:23,320
the Yangtze River.

54
00:04:23,321 --> 00:04:26,620
Earthen dams diverted the river's flow around the construction site.

55
00:04:26,621 --> 00:04:32,020
In the case of the Yangtze River, it's the third largest river in the world.

56
00:04:32,021 --> 00:04:38,180
It needs to be completely diverted for a period of over ten years while the dam is being built.

57
00:04:38,181 --> 00:04:45,120
Holding back the flood-prone Yangtze River has long been a top priority for the Chinese.

58
00:04:45,121 --> 00:04:54,820
Over the last two millennia, the Yangtze River has averaged a major flood once every 10 years.

59
00:04:54,821 --> 00:05:00,880
But in 1918, it wasn't flood control, but hydroelectricity that compelled China's revolutionary

60
00:05:00,881 --> 00:05:07,240
leader Sun Yat-sen to promote a Three Gorges Dam project.

61
00:05:07,241 --> 00:05:13,560
The country's instability sidelined the project until floods in the 1930s and 1950s killed

62
00:05:13,561 --> 00:05:18,620
hundreds of thousands of Chinese, spurring Chinese officials to begin planning in earnest

63
00:05:18,621 --> 00:05:26,560
for a dam with a massive reservoir that could reduce flooding and produce electricity.

64
00:05:26,561 --> 00:05:31,160
Because of the enormity of the project, the dam remained in the planning stages until

65
00:05:31,161 --> 00:05:37,200
the People's Congress approved it in 1992.

66
00:05:37,201 --> 00:05:43,860
The plans called for a 607-foot-high, 1.4-mile-long concrete gravity dam, with two powerhouses

67
00:05:43,861 --> 00:05:50,300
flanking a central spillway capable of passing 3.5 million cubic feet per second of the river's

68
00:05:50,301 --> 00:05:53,440
water.

69
00:05:53,441 --> 00:05:59,160
Three Gorges Dam would contain 35 million cubic yards of concrete, enough to lay a 16-foot-wide

70
00:05:59,161 --> 00:06:03,320
highway across the U.S. more than 10 times.

71
00:06:03,321 --> 00:06:09,920
In 1997, with the diversion canal keeping the site dry, workers began pouring concrete.

72
00:06:09,921 --> 00:06:17,320
In a dam, you start by creating what's called lifts of two to three to four meters where

73
00:06:17,321 --> 00:06:21,700
forms are put on the side to keep the concrete in a certain designated area.

74
00:06:21,960 --> 00:06:24,020
Then concrete's poured in that area.

75
00:06:25,460 --> 00:06:31,420
After pouring concrete for 10 years, workers finished the main structure of the dam in 2006.

76
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:39,300
An estimated 250,000 people have worked on this $22.5 billion project,

77
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:44,240
the costliest and perhaps largest construction project in modern history.

78
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:52,740
Many times big projects in developing countries are built by large American or European companies,

79
00:06:52,741 --> 00:06:56,280
but the Chinese decided they wanted to build this mostly on their own

80
00:06:56,281 --> 00:06:59,760
with help in specialized areas from Western engineering companies.

81
00:06:59,761 --> 00:07:07,860
With the dam structure complete, the river was returned to its natural course and impounded

82
00:07:07,861 --> 00:07:14,320
in the 400-mile-long reservoir behind the dam, inundating 13 cities, 140 towns, and

83
00:07:14,321 --> 00:07:15,760
almost 1,400 villages.

84
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:24,040
The most dramatic chapter in an ongoing relocation of an estimated 1.4 million people.

85
00:07:24,041 --> 00:07:31,040
Some experts predict that relocation costs will equal the cost of the dam itself.

86
00:07:31,041 --> 00:07:38,040
Adding to the controversy surrounding the dam is the fact that its reservoir will flood

87
00:07:38,041 --> 00:07:43,040
countless sites of historical and archaeological importance and inundate some of the area's

88
00:07:43,041 --> 00:07:45,040
most fertile farmland.

89
00:07:45,041 --> 00:07:51,040
But when the 26 four-ton turbines are in place and generating electricity, they will supply

90
00:07:51,041 --> 00:07:53,700
they will supply 3% of China's power needs.

91
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:59,200
Dams may be the construction giants of the 20th and now 21st centuries,

92
00:07:59,900 --> 00:08:02,520
but their impact on civilization is ancient.

93
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:09,540
5,000 years ago, Mesopotamians developed this new technology, the dam,

94
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:13,320
to transform the desert into what would be known as the Fertile Crescent.

95
00:08:14,900 --> 00:08:16,860
Mesopotamians used embankment dams,

96
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:20,900
earthen and stone structures that hold back water with their mass

97
00:08:20,901 --> 00:08:24,660
to divert water into irrigation canals that fed their fields.

98
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:29,400
As dam technology spread throughout the region,

99
00:08:30,020 --> 00:08:31,640
the dams themselves began to grow.

100
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:37,080
Civilizations attempted to store water in reservoirs, not just divert it.

101
00:08:37,640 --> 00:08:41,280
Diversion dams are the earliest, but you don't store a lot.

102
00:08:41,281 --> 00:08:44,100
You're still dependent upon the natural flow of the river.

103
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:49,660
As dams get taller, you have the capacity to actually store the water,

104
00:08:49,661 --> 00:08:53,000
to capture those spring floods, those freshets,

105
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:55,200
and then use it later in the year.

106
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,440
But holding back a reservoir with just the weight of earth and rocks

107
00:08:59,441 --> 00:09:01,160
takes massive amounts of material.

108
00:09:02,600 --> 00:09:05,300
The Romans engineered a way to do more with less.

109
00:09:07,340 --> 00:09:09,980
If you took a masonry wall

110
00:09:09,981 --> 00:09:13,140
and didn't build it straight across a canyon,

111
00:09:13,620 --> 00:09:15,380
but you built it with an arch,

112
00:09:15,381 --> 00:09:21,660
that you could actually use the strength of the arch to help hold back the water.

113
00:09:22,780 --> 00:09:26,300
But the arch is only as strong as the foundation into which it's anchored.

114
00:09:27,240 --> 00:09:30,220
Arches were suited to narrow areas with solid rock foundations.

115
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:35,940
The next innovation, buttress dams, were more versatile.

116
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:39,760
They spread their load across a series of buttresses,

117
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:42,680
didn't require massive amounts of fill material,

118
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:44,760
and could span wider areas.

119
00:09:45,380 --> 00:09:52,380
Despite these innovations, the most common dam is still the Earthen Embankment Dam.

120
00:09:52,381 --> 00:09:59,380
It's the dam of choice in areas where the dam site is wide, and there are large amounts of Earthville available.

121
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:06,380
An Embankment Dam is the tallest in the United States.

122
00:10:06,381 --> 00:10:13,380
The 770-foot-tall Oroville Dam on the Feather River in Northern California was built in

123
00:10:13,381 --> 00:10:19,600
the 1960s from clay and cobblestone, recycled from an abandoned gold mine.

124
00:10:19,601 --> 00:10:25,700
By 1963, workers had diverted the river with two tunnels and cleared the site to bedrock.

125
00:10:25,701 --> 00:10:30,420
Next they began placing earth film.

126
00:10:30,421 --> 00:10:35,100
Oroville Dam is a gravity dam that holds back water with its mass.

127
00:10:35,101 --> 00:10:39,620
The core is composed of impermeable clay, surrounded by a transition layer of coarser

128
00:10:39,621 --> 00:10:46,080
material and covered in rock, sand and gravel.

129
00:10:46,081 --> 00:10:51,280
The weight of the structure is simply too great for the reservoir to push aside.

130
00:10:51,281 --> 00:10:59,000
During construction, bucket wheel excavators dug out 3,500 cubic yards of fill per hour.

131
00:10:59,001 --> 00:11:03,740
Workers transported the fill on conveyor belts to the train hopper, where they loaded 40

132
00:11:03,741 --> 00:11:08,420
train cars every 15 minutes.

133
00:11:08,421 --> 00:11:12,820
Three locomotives continually carted the 80 million cubic yards of earth material to

134
00:11:12,821 --> 00:11:19,660
the site where workers sorted it, dumped it into the dam, and packed it down.

135
00:11:19,661 --> 00:11:24,420
Completed in seven years, the Oroville Dam supplies electricity, drinking water, and

136
00:11:24,421 --> 00:11:29,800
irrigation for central and southern California.

137
00:11:29,801 --> 00:11:33,976
reservoir and spillway also provide flood control.

138
00:11:33,977 --> 00:11:36,900
When open, eight 30-foot tall gates

139
00:11:36,901 --> 00:11:43,420
are capable of releasing 250,000 cubic feet of water per second to the river below.

140
00:11:43,421 --> 00:11:50,359
Oroville, the 19th largest dam in the world, dwarfs most dams in the U.S.

141
00:11:50,360 --> 00:11:51,880
Still, engineers

142
00:11:51,881 --> 00:11:57,760
are retrofitting many smaller water storage and irrigation dams with hydropower, finding

143
00:11:57,761 --> 00:12:01,760
ways to make more electricity from lesser dams.

144
00:12:01,761 --> 00:12:10,460
In the 20th century, dam building displaced as many as 80 million people worldwide.

145
00:12:10,461 --> 00:12:12,600
Dams will return on Modern Marvels.

146
00:12:12,601 --> 00:12:19,840
At Rock Island Dam on the Columbia River in the heart of Washington State, a test of strength

147
00:12:19,841 --> 00:12:20,840
is taking place.

148
00:12:20,841 --> 00:12:24,340
No, it's not dam versus river.

149
00:12:24,341 --> 00:12:28,020
This facility has withstood the water's pressure for more than 70 years.

150
00:12:28,021 --> 00:12:36,760
But today, deep within Powerhouse One, a decade-long modernization is about to begin, and it starts

151
00:12:36,761 --> 00:12:40,720
with a deadlift.

152
00:12:40,721 --> 00:12:47,060
Workers will have to hoist the Rotor, a 271,000-pound steel and copper assembly, out of the top

153
00:12:47,061 --> 00:12:49,920
of the generating unit.

154
00:12:49,921 --> 00:12:56,080
In each one of these 10 power units, the rotor, a rotating electromagnet, spins inside a stationary

155
00:12:56,081 --> 00:12:59,920
stator which is packed with conductive copper wiring.

156
00:12:59,921 --> 00:13:04,660
The rotor's spinning electromagnet carries a charge that causes electrons to move from

157
00:13:04,661 --> 00:13:10,260
atom to atom in the stator's wiring, producing the electron flow called electricity.

158
00:13:10,261 --> 00:13:16,720
There's a huge magnetic force of about a million pounds that tries to draw these two

159
00:13:16,721 --> 00:13:18,920
magnets together.

160
00:13:18,921 --> 00:13:24,300
So engineers work to design systems that can keep that rotor spinning it up to 90 RPMs

161
00:13:24,301 --> 00:13:27,640
perfectly inside the stator.

162
00:13:27,641 --> 00:13:33,040
As the equipment ages and bearings wear down, the rotor begins to wobble and runs the risk

163
00:13:33,041 --> 00:13:37,160
of banging into the stator, potentially destroying the machine.

164
00:13:37,161 --> 00:13:40,620
So it must be pulled out and upgraded.

165
00:13:40,621 --> 00:13:48,020
An operator positions two bridge cranes, each capable of hoisting 80 tons above the rotor.

166
00:13:48,021 --> 00:13:53,900
is delicate work the rotor must be perfectly level as it's coming out

167
00:13:54,040 --> 00:13:59,820
there's about an eighth of an inch gap between the rotor and the stator and as

168
00:13:59,821 --> 00:14:03,620
they've been lifting the rotor up because it's not level it's been coming

169
00:14:03,621 --> 00:14:07,900
in contact and hanging up on the edge of the stator

170
00:14:08,080 --> 00:14:14,260
finally the crane operator levels the huge rotor enough to extract it

171
00:14:14,261 --> 00:14:25,860
The cranes cart the load the length of a powerhouse and gingerly set it down.

172
00:14:25,861 --> 00:14:30,500
This unit was originally installed in about 1953, and this is the first time it's been

173
00:14:30,501 --> 00:14:34,480
out, so it's been in service for well over 50 years.

174
00:14:34,481 --> 00:14:38,700
Over the years, insulation and copper windings have worn down.

175
00:14:38,701 --> 00:14:43,560
From exposure to heat, vibration, moisture and oil, wear and tear decrease efficiency

176
00:14:43,561 --> 00:14:44,960
and can shut the generator down.

177
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:47,940
The rotor will sit there for about four months

178
00:14:47,941 --> 00:14:51,200
while work crews remove the old copper laminations

179
00:14:51,201 --> 00:14:55,420
and install new copper laminations of improved purity and design.

180
00:14:58,060 --> 00:15:01,240
The Rock Island modernization will take 10 years

181
00:15:01,241 --> 00:15:02,960
and cost $180 million.

182
00:15:03,620 --> 00:15:07,300
The project will extend the life of the powerhouse another 50 years.

183
00:15:08,320 --> 00:15:12,340
After the upgrade, the Dames power plant will produce 10% more energy

184
00:15:12,341 --> 00:15:13,660
from the same amount of water.

185
00:15:16,300 --> 00:15:19,420
This idea, using dams to generate electricity,

186
00:15:20,080 --> 00:15:21,860
began at the end of the 19th century

187
00:15:21,861 --> 00:15:23,960
with the advent of power lines

188
00:15:23,961 --> 00:15:26,100
that distributed electricity over distances.

189
00:15:27,940 --> 00:15:30,820
Private utilities began to build hydroelectric dams,

190
00:15:31,200 --> 00:15:32,340
even in remote areas.

191
00:15:32,740 --> 00:15:34,540
The coming of hydroelectricity

192
00:15:34,541 --> 00:15:36,560
and the coming of electric power systems

193
00:15:36,561 --> 00:15:38,660
in the 1880s and the 1890s

194
00:15:38,661 --> 00:15:40,700
really has a huge impact

195
00:15:40,701 --> 00:15:46,700
on why people want to begin to build dams in many, many different locations.

196
00:15:46,701 --> 00:15:51,700
Over the next hundred years, states and industries hungry for water and power

197
00:15:51,701 --> 00:15:55,700
began damming up virtually every major river in the United States.

198
00:15:55,701 --> 00:16:01,700
In the 1930s, President Roosevelt, in an effort to stimulate the depressed economy,

199
00:16:01,701 --> 00:16:05,700
authorized the building of some of the country's largest dam projects,

200
00:16:05,701 --> 00:16:09,700
including the Shasta, Bonneville, and Grand Coulee Dams.

201
00:16:09,701 --> 00:16:20,700
Decade by decade over the next 40 years, the number of dams built would skyrocket.

202
00:16:20,701 --> 00:16:28,700
1,400 dams built in the 1930s would spike to 8,000 built in the 1970s.

203
00:16:28,701 --> 00:16:36,700
Many of the smaller dams were designed for water storage and irrigation, not hydropower.

204
00:16:36,701 --> 00:16:41,800
Today, the high cost of electricity has spurred interest in adding hydropower to these smaller dams.

205
00:16:42,980 --> 00:16:45,040
They tend to be low head.

206
00:16:45,160 --> 00:16:50,720
That means the difference in elevation from the upstream to the downstream of the dam is not so high.

207
00:16:51,100 --> 00:16:54,900
And these were not so economical to develop and to extract power from these dams,

208
00:16:54,920 --> 00:16:58,020
which were often there for water storage or for navigation.

209
00:16:58,021 --> 00:17:03,340
But today, there is an interest in developing special designs for these low head dams.

210
00:17:03,341 --> 00:17:10,020
Each project has a unique height of the dam, a unique amount of water versus the time of the year.

211
00:17:10,021 --> 00:17:16,060
And so to match each of those variable conditions, each plant is designed exactly for its condition.

212
00:17:17,580 --> 00:17:23,160
The lowerhead dams on the Mississippi and Ohio rivers require a compact power plant.

213
00:17:24,040 --> 00:17:25,840
There isn't much depth at these dams.

214
00:17:27,260 --> 00:17:32,380
As a result, axial flow bulb turbines have been designed to be installed horizontally.

215
00:17:33,340 --> 00:17:36,620
with the turbine and generator all packed into one compact unit.

216
00:17:38,060 --> 00:17:42,860
It basically has a central hub with the blades welded to the hub.

217
00:17:42,861 --> 00:17:46,940
This whole runner is also made 100% of stainless steel.

218
00:17:46,941 --> 00:17:54,460
This design will go into a powerhouse structure that has a lot of water flow, but very small head.

219
00:17:55,820 --> 00:17:59,900
At Danswith Higher Head, in the 75 to 1200 foot range,

220
00:17:59,901 --> 00:18:07,880
engineers employ a Francis turbine these turbines have fixed blades welded at the top and bottom to

221
00:18:07,881 --> 00:18:14,020
a crown and a base Francis turbines have greater structural rigidity to withstand increased water

222
00:18:14,021 --> 00:18:20,300
pressures at these higher head downs the water actually comes in and makes contact directly

223
00:18:20,301 --> 00:18:27,380
with this surface of the bucket kind of like a prize fighter when he hits his opponent he wants

224
00:18:27,381 --> 00:18:28,880
to hit his opponent squarely.

225
00:18:28,881 --> 00:18:31,700
If he hits his opponent at a glancing blow,

226
00:18:31,701 --> 00:18:33,620
he doesn't transfer that energy.

227
00:18:33,621 --> 00:18:35,820
The water operates on the same principle

228
00:18:35,821 --> 00:18:37,580
with these buckets.

229
00:18:37,581 --> 00:18:41,120
The water conveyance system would involve the pen stock,

230
00:18:41,121 --> 00:18:43,880
delivering the water into a spiral case.

231
00:18:43,881 --> 00:18:46,400
And the spiral case looks like a snail shell,

232
00:18:46,401 --> 00:18:48,380
where it's bigger at the opening, and it

233
00:18:48,381 --> 00:18:51,200
volutes into a smaller diameter as it

234
00:18:51,201 --> 00:18:54,380
wraps around the machine.

235
00:18:54,381 --> 00:18:57,360
This accelerates the water into the Francis Runner.

236
00:18:57,361 --> 00:19:02,940
which weighs 56,000 pounds, spins anywhere from 150 to 250 RPM,

237
00:19:03,760 --> 00:19:05,240
and must be perfectly balanced.

238
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:08,880
A giant lathe slowly shaves it down.

239
00:19:09,540 --> 00:19:13,280
We remove metal in designated areas on the runner

240
00:19:13,281 --> 00:19:15,100
so that the runner is balanced.

241
00:19:15,980 --> 00:19:19,660
These massive turbines cost up to $10 million to manufacture,

242
00:19:20,200 --> 00:19:23,940
and as much as $1 million just to ship to the dam site

243
00:19:23,941 --> 00:19:25,200
where they will be installed.

244
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:33,320
But energy from hydropower is extremely valuable because water power is flexible.

245
00:19:34,320 --> 00:19:39,720
One of the great advantages of hydro is you can turn it on and off relatively quickly.

246
00:19:40,040 --> 00:19:49,420
If you have a big coal-fired steam plant and you get that baby going, you can't turn that off very easily.

247
00:19:49,421 --> 00:19:54,140
It might take days to cool down, and it's going to take days to heat back up.

248
00:19:54,141 --> 00:20:02,420
With hydropower, you can actually turn those valves and in a matter of minutes go from nothing up to full power.

249
00:20:02,940 --> 00:20:04,600
And that's its great advantage.

250
00:20:05,020 --> 00:20:11,540
But when these hydroelectric barriers block once free-flowing rivers, how can ships and fish get around the dams?

251
00:20:13,620 --> 00:20:17,360
Hydropower is responsible for about 20% of the world's electricity.

252
00:20:18,340 --> 00:20:20,540
Dams will return on Modern Marvels.

253
00:20:20,541 --> 00:20:27,300
Today, the Columbia River is a vital shipping route, flowing from the Canadian Rockies in

254
00:20:27,301 --> 00:20:31,500
British Columbia through Washington and Oregon.

255
00:20:31,501 --> 00:20:36,580
Dams have made this possible, but only with some clever engineering.

256
00:20:36,581 --> 00:20:44,280
The Columbia River is the United States' largest gateway for the export of wheat.

257
00:20:44,281 --> 00:20:47,960
About 8 to 10 million tons of cargo each year moves on the river.

258
00:20:47,961 --> 00:20:52,960
It's valued somewhere between $2 and $2.5 billion a year.

259
00:20:52,961 --> 00:20:59,400
The dams on the Columbia River in Washington State, like Rock Island Dam and Grand Coulee

260
00:20:59,401 --> 00:21:04,440
Dam, don't have locks and don't allow ship passage.

261
00:21:04,441 --> 00:21:10,980
But just to their south, eight dams, each with a lock, transform the Columbia and its

262
00:21:10,981 --> 00:21:17,580
tributary the Snake River into a water highway spanning 465 miles from the

263
00:21:17,581 --> 00:21:21,480
Pacific Ocean to Lewiston, Idaho.

264
00:21:21,481 --> 00:21:23,480
Okay, starting to come ahead here in a minute.

265
00:21:23,481 --> 00:21:28,700
A tug pushes four barges, each one the length of a football field, up the

266
00:21:28,701 --> 00:21:32,480
Columbia River towards the navigation lock at Bonneville Dam.

267
00:21:32,481 --> 00:21:35,980
I'm open up the downstream gates,

268
00:21:35,981 --> 00:21:42,100
the green light for them to come in and at that point he's almost dead stop

269
00:21:42,101 --> 00:21:47,500
otherwise he would just ran right into the wall on dry land you wouldn't want

270
00:21:47,501 --> 00:21:53,500
to negotiate a parking spot this tight 650 feet is the largest toe you can put

271
00:21:53,501 --> 00:21:59,220
in and you might have you know a foot two feet to spare on either end so it's

272
00:21:59,221 --> 00:22:03,860
pretty tight it may be tight but this lock is the only way to lift boats up

273
00:22:03,861 --> 00:22:07,760
60 feet and over the dam.

274
00:22:07,761 --> 00:22:10,940
Once I get the gates closed, I miter the gates,

275
00:22:10,941 --> 00:22:14,780
pushing them together, and then I start filling the water

276
00:22:14,781 --> 00:22:16,520
by operating in two tainter valves.

277
00:22:16,521 --> 00:22:19,440
Now these tainter valves are valves that come open

278
00:22:19,441 --> 00:22:22,460
and lets the water come in, run in through the bottom,

279
00:22:22,461 --> 00:22:24,600
fill up the locks.

280
00:22:24,601 --> 00:22:28,020
The lock is a glorified bathtub.

281
00:22:28,021 --> 00:22:31,980
You have a large, empty structure.

282
00:22:31,981 --> 00:22:33,440
You have gates on either end.

283
00:22:34,080 --> 00:22:35,420
There are no pumps involved.

284
00:22:35,520 --> 00:22:36,740
This is all gravity.

285
00:22:37,960 --> 00:22:40,560
But if it's a bathtub, it's a massive one.

286
00:22:41,220 --> 00:22:43,200
The lock at Bonneville Dam takes 10 minutes

287
00:22:43,201 --> 00:22:47,240
and needs a staggering 30 million gallons of water each time it fills.

288
00:22:48,260 --> 00:22:50,960
Once it gets them up, I open the upstream gates,

289
00:22:51,680 --> 00:22:54,720
give them a horn, a green light, and verbally tell them they're cleared to go.

290
00:22:57,560 --> 00:23:01,680
You'll see the agricultural and forestry products coming downstream

291
00:23:01,681 --> 00:23:04,820
going upstream, fuel, fertilizers.

292
00:23:05,440 --> 00:23:09,020
The cost involved in that is so minimal

293
00:23:09,021 --> 00:23:12,280
compared to if you had to put them all on trains

294
00:23:12,281 --> 00:23:14,040
or if you had to put it all on trucks.

295
00:23:16,140 --> 00:23:18,680
And you'd have to if it weren't for Bonneville Dam.

296
00:23:19,980 --> 00:23:22,780
Built over a five-year period during the Great Depression,

297
00:23:23,660 --> 00:23:25,600
the dam deepened this rocky narrows,

298
00:23:26,180 --> 00:23:27,440
making it passable for shipping.

299
00:23:27,441 --> 00:23:36,440
Bonneville Dam is a concrete gravity dam that measures 180 feet thick at its base.

300
00:23:36,441 --> 00:23:41,440
Size-wise, the two powerhouses are roughly a quarter of a mile long a piece,

301
00:23:41,441 --> 00:23:43,440
same thing with the spillway.

302
00:23:43,441 --> 00:23:50,440
We have a little less than 2 million cubic yards of concrete between the three structures.

303
00:23:50,441 --> 00:23:55,440
We generate about a little over a thousand megawatts of power.

304
00:23:55,441 --> 00:23:58,540
The largest dam in the area, of course, is Grand Coulee.

305
00:23:58,860 --> 00:24:01,220
They produce six times what we have.

306
00:24:01,980 --> 00:24:07,580
Completed in 1941, Grand Coulee is the largest concrete dam in the United States

307
00:24:07,581 --> 00:24:11,540
and typifies the multipurpose role of the dams on the Columbia River.

308
00:24:12,200 --> 00:24:15,860
The dam is used for power production, flood control, and irrigation.

309
00:24:16,520 --> 00:24:22,920
Though beneficial to people, the dams on the Columbia River are damaging some of the river's original inhabitants,

310
00:24:22,921 --> 00:24:27,160
fish, and in particular, the region's salmon populations.

311
00:24:30,600 --> 00:24:34,000
Salmon hatch in the river, migrate to the ocean to mature,

312
00:24:34,480 --> 00:24:36,020
and return up the river to spawn.

313
00:24:37,460 --> 00:24:40,000
The dams present a huge obstacle to migration,

314
00:24:40,700 --> 00:24:44,520
particularly to the small juveniles or fingerlings heading out to sea.

315
00:24:46,660 --> 00:24:51,280
At each dam, you have a very high percentage of juveniles dying,

316
00:24:51,281 --> 00:24:52,220
and it's additive.

317
00:24:52,221 --> 00:25:00,220
So if you're looking at 10 to 15% at each dam, you've already lost a really large chunk of your potential run size before it even goes to the ocean.

318
00:25:02,180 --> 00:25:09,960
In an effort to reduce losses, the Army Corps of Engineers has installed a number of structures to protect fish, including fish screens.

319
00:25:11,040 --> 00:25:12,980
Submerge traveling screens.

320
00:25:13,320 --> 00:25:20,580
They are large structures that slide down in the upstream side of the dam, and then they angle out, and then they have a motor on them.

321
00:25:20,581 --> 00:25:24,800
The fish come down, catch the current, and then they're directed into a bypass system.

322
00:25:25,160 --> 00:25:31,280
The bypass system simply is water flowing around the structure rather than through the structure,

323
00:25:31,520 --> 00:25:33,120
and then they're deposited downstream.

324
00:25:35,860 --> 00:25:38,720
During this process of moving the fish around the dams,

325
00:25:39,180 --> 00:25:41,700
workers at the dams are able to collect about half of them,

326
00:25:42,320 --> 00:25:46,260
which are then loaded into barges and shipped downriver directly to the ocean.

327
00:25:46,261 --> 00:25:53,960
Marging reduces fish mortality at the dams, but trauma experienced en route may simply

328
00:25:53,961 --> 00:26:00,160
lead to a later death in the ocean.

329
00:26:00,161 --> 00:26:07,340
Adults returning to spawn can use man-made fish ladders to pass over the dam.

330
00:26:07,341 --> 00:26:11,760
At each of the dams there is a flow that comes through the ladders that attracts the fish.

331
00:26:11,761 --> 00:26:17,880
The fish then, in essence, are swimming through a small man-made stream, rising two or three

332
00:26:17,881 --> 00:26:23,560
feet at a time, moving up the hundred-foot rise in most of the dams on the Columbia Snake

333
00:26:23,561 --> 00:26:24,560
System.

334
00:26:24,561 --> 00:26:29,140
Fish ladder is a path that allows you to go around the dam.

335
00:26:29,141 --> 00:26:34,880
You get fish waiting down at the bottom for a chance to get up, and they're using up their

336
00:26:34,881 --> 00:26:41,440
energy there, and then they have 20 or 30 miles of slack water lake to swim through.

337
00:26:41,441 --> 00:26:47,100
When I first got to this project in the early 1980s, hydropower was king.

338
00:26:47,101 --> 00:26:51,280
If something interfered with hydropower, we didn't do it.

339
00:26:51,281 --> 00:26:53,720
Now fish is king.

340
00:26:53,721 --> 00:26:56,360
And if it interferes with fish, we don't do it.

341
00:26:56,361 --> 00:27:00,940
If that means shutting off a powerhouse, that means shutting off a powerhouse.

342
00:27:00,941 --> 00:27:04,640
But why has fish welfare become so important?

343
00:27:04,641 --> 00:27:09,220
The main reason, of course, is the Endangered Species Act.

344
00:27:09,221 --> 00:27:11,987
of course gives it a lot of clout.

345
00:27:11,988 --> 00:27:16,220
So with the runs getting dangerously low, every fish

346
00:27:16,740 --> 00:27:18,400
is important.

347
00:27:18,401 --> 00:27:23,280
There is tremendous amount of effort going in on the part of the various agencies to

348
00:27:23,281 --> 00:27:26,335
make sure that fish can get up and down past these dams.

349
00:27:26,336 --> 00:27:28,080
But at the end of the day, we've

350
00:27:28,081 --> 00:27:33,400
got a lot of runs that are upstream of the dams that are not doing well and there's problems

351
00:27:33,401 --> 00:27:34,400
with them.

352
00:27:34,401 --> 00:27:37,540
But the big dams are probably here to stay.

353
00:27:38,520 --> 00:27:42,880
There's little debate that hydropower is far cleaner than electricity derived from fossil fuels.

354
00:27:45,320 --> 00:27:53,020
As many as one million adult salmon and steelhead and 50 million fingerlings pass through Bonneville Dam in an average year.

355
00:27:54,380 --> 00:27:57,060
Dams will return on Modern Marvels.

356
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:02,700
Flood.

357
00:28:02,701 --> 00:28:06,680
It's one of nature's most potent destroyers of life and property.

358
00:28:06,681 --> 00:28:12,220
In an area of frequent flooding, one of a dam's key selling points is its ability to

359
00:28:12,221 --> 00:28:14,280
restrain an onslaught of water.

360
00:28:14,281 --> 00:28:19,660
While we designed the dam to hold a certain amount of water, we also designed features

361
00:28:19,661 --> 00:28:24,360
into the dam that allow us to pass excess water around that dam.

362
00:28:24,361 --> 00:28:30,680
So a big part is understanding how large is the flood that we would have to handle through

363
00:28:30,681 --> 00:28:36,600
some sort of a spillway in order to safely pass that water around the dam during a storm event

364
00:28:40,120 --> 00:28:43,800
but there are rare times when the dam itself is the cause of the flood

365
00:28:46,440 --> 00:28:54,040
at 10 a.m on june 5th 1976 workers in southeastern idaho tried desperately to close an ever widening

366
00:28:54,041 --> 00:28:58,540
fissure in the downstream face of the Teton Dam.

367
00:28:58,541 --> 00:29:00,940
But the growing hole consumed their bulldozers.

368
00:29:04,340 --> 00:29:09,280
At 1157 AM, the crest of the 305-foot earthen dam

369
00:29:09,281 --> 00:29:12,900
collapsed as its reservoir was being filled for the first time.

370
00:29:15,720 --> 00:29:17,980
80 billion gallons of water crashed down

371
00:29:17,981 --> 00:29:19,840
on the farming communities below the dam.

372
00:29:24,040 --> 00:29:34,040
Directly downstream, five communities, Rexburg, Sugar City, Wilford, Salem, and Hibbard, were swept away.

373
00:29:34,041 --> 00:29:38,040
The wall of water ripped buildings from their foundations.

374
00:29:38,041 --> 00:29:41,040
80% of Rexburg was inundated and damaged.

375
00:29:41,041 --> 00:29:46,040
The riverbank community of Sugar City virtually disappeared.

376
00:29:46,041 --> 00:29:54,660
The flood killed 11 people and 16,000 head of livestock and caused hundreds of millions

377
00:29:54,661 --> 00:29:57,900
of dollars in property damage.

378
00:29:57,901 --> 00:30:05,980
Loss of life would have been far greater had there not been time for an evacuation.

379
00:30:05,981 --> 00:30:10,380
The Teton Dam had been designed by the Bureau of Reclamation, a division of the Department

380
00:30:10,381 --> 00:30:15,400
of the Interior, now more than a century old.

381
00:30:15,401 --> 00:30:20,220
The Bureau oversees water development projects in the western United States and has built

382
00:30:20,221 --> 00:30:25,560
some 600 dams, including the Hoover and Grand Coulee.

383
00:30:25,561 --> 00:30:29,920
Teton's collapse marks the lone failure among the Bureau's major dams.

384
00:30:29,921 --> 00:30:33,440
It led to an investigation that revealed three main causes of the catastrophe.

385
00:30:33,441 --> 00:30:36,900
There were open fissures in the foundation.

386
00:30:36,901 --> 00:30:42,240
Those conditions could have had more attention given to them during the design of the dam.

387
00:30:42,241 --> 00:30:45,280
There was a very challenging core material.

388
00:30:45,281 --> 00:30:51,100
The core material that was available locally was a silk material, which is highly erosive,

389
00:30:51,101 --> 00:30:53,840
and so it has to be very carefully protected.

390
00:30:54,760 --> 00:31:02,100
And really the third contributing factor was not having some defensive measures that we now use on a regular basis

391
00:31:02,101 --> 00:31:08,540
when we're designing embankment dams, namely being filters, to keep the embankment materials where they belong.

392
00:31:08,541 --> 00:31:15,540
The Teton incident had an impact on the federal government, understanding that it had this

393
00:31:15,541 --> 00:31:21,540
tremendous infrastructure that needed to be cared for, it needed to be paid attention to.

394
00:31:21,541 --> 00:31:26,540
Caring for aging western dams is one of the main missions of the Bureau of Reclamation's

395
00:31:26,541 --> 00:31:33,540
engineers at their Water Resources Research Laboratory in Denver, Colorado.

396
00:31:33,541 --> 00:31:39,420
At this massive facility, they test ways to mitigate the host of factors that can cause a dam to give way.

397
00:31:42,420 --> 00:31:44,020
Water is incredibly heavy.

398
00:31:44,980 --> 00:31:47,240
A single gallon weighs in at more than 8 pounds.

399
00:31:49,020 --> 00:31:51,640
Dams direct their reservoir loads into their foundations.

400
00:31:52,500 --> 00:31:59,040
The water exerts 17,000 pounds of pressure per square foot onto the base of a dam the size of Teton Dam.

401
00:31:59,041 --> 00:32:06,600
You have to take a look at the potential weaknesses in that foundation that would allow some sort

402
00:32:06,601 --> 00:32:11,800
of slippage that would then allow a piece or all of the dam to slide downstream and

403
00:32:11,801 --> 00:32:13,800
release the reservoir.

404
00:32:13,801 --> 00:32:21,080
You may find that there are storms possible that were never envisioned by the original

405
00:32:21,081 --> 00:32:26,440
designers, which in that case would lead to an overtopping of the dam and erosion of the

406
00:32:26,441 --> 00:32:28,320
downstream face.

407
00:32:28,321 --> 00:32:33,600
Over topping, excess water spilling over the top of a dam, is the most common cause of

408
00:32:33,601 --> 00:32:35,600
dam collapse.

409
00:32:35,601 --> 00:32:43,220
Spillways are really our primary feature for managing the release of water around the dam

410
00:32:43,221 --> 00:32:45,740
in a flood event particularly.

411
00:32:45,741 --> 00:32:51,080
The spillway is basically a safe channel to carry the water around the dam and get it

412
00:32:51,081 --> 00:32:55,360
back into the watercourse downstream.

413
00:32:55,361 --> 00:33:00,920
At their Denver facility, Bureau engineers are testing a fuse plug, an earthen gate designed

414
00:33:00,921 --> 00:33:04,240
to give way and allow water to pass in a flood.

415
00:33:04,241 --> 00:33:09,760
The water normally doesn't go through the spillway unless there's a big flood, and when

416
00:33:09,761 --> 00:33:16,220
there is a big flood, each one of the individual embankments will wash away in a prescribed

417
00:33:16,221 --> 00:33:20,420
manner in order to open up that section of spillway.

418
00:33:20,421 --> 00:33:25,600
With 90% of its dams nearing the half-century mark, the Bureau of Reclamation tests the

419
00:33:25,601 --> 00:33:29,300
strength of the concrete in each dam every 10 or 15 years.

420
00:33:29,301 --> 00:33:34,420
A good durable concrete will continue to strengthen as time goes on.

421
00:33:34,421 --> 00:33:40,840
In some instances there are things like freezing and thawing or abrasion, and we need to look

422
00:33:40,841 --> 00:33:45,540
at those items and monitor them over time.

423
00:33:45,541 --> 00:33:50,220
continues to slowly hydrate for years, absorbing water and forming crystal

424
00:33:50,221 --> 00:33:55,860
lattices that give it strength. Over a 50-year period, the concrete in a dam may

425
00:33:55,861 --> 00:34:02,160
become as much as 250% stronger. The smaller cores we usually test in our

426
00:34:02,161 --> 00:34:06,780
300,000 pound machine. In there today we have a core from our upper Stillwater

427
00:34:06,781 --> 00:34:11,780
Dam and this course was taken from our 15-year testing program. Right now it's

428
00:34:11,781 --> 00:34:13,040
about 20 years old.

429
00:34:13,041 --> 00:34:16,680
The point of the test is to test in compression

430
00:34:16,681 --> 00:34:18,920
until we find the ultimate compressive strength,

431
00:34:18,921 --> 00:34:20,020
until it fails.

432
00:34:20,021 --> 00:34:22,600
And that's the maximum load that we can get out

433
00:34:22,601 --> 00:34:25,260
of that concrete.

434
00:34:25,261 --> 00:34:27,920
A compressive strength test will measure how much pressure

435
00:34:27,921 --> 00:34:29,360
it takes to crush this core.

436
00:34:32,800 --> 00:34:36,240
This cylinder just broke at 7,000 psi,

437
00:34:36,241 --> 00:34:39,500
which is slightly higher than we were expecting,

438
00:34:39,501 --> 00:34:43,300
which shows us that it's continuing to gain strength over time.

439
00:34:46,020 --> 00:34:50,220
Dam failures, while catastrophic, are also unparalleled learning opportunities.

440
00:34:50,740 --> 00:34:53,220
We had a significant incident with Teton Dam.

441
00:34:53,660 --> 00:34:55,980
We feel like we've learned some tremendous lessons.

442
00:34:56,800 --> 00:34:59,060
And as a result of learning those lessons,

443
00:34:59,061 --> 00:35:03,160
we have been very active in trying to share those lessons with others,

444
00:35:03,680 --> 00:35:06,520
both throughout the United States and throughout the world,

445
00:35:06,720 --> 00:35:08,840
to try to further the cause of dam safety.

446
00:35:09,500 --> 00:35:16,500
Bureau of Reclamation dams provide irrigation water for 140,000 farmers in the American

447
00:35:16,501 --> 00:35:21,620
West, who produce 60% of the nation's vegetables.

448
00:35:21,621 --> 00:35:27,820
Dams will return on Modern Marvels.

449
00:35:27,821 --> 00:35:31,900
Beaver dams can have a dramatic, beneficial effect on their surroundings.

450
00:35:31,901 --> 00:35:37,200
In many ways, these rudimentary constructions, built to create ponds that protect beavers

451
00:35:37,201 --> 00:35:43,860
from predators are ecologically far superior to their man-made counterparts.

452
00:35:43,861 --> 00:35:48,940
A beaver dam has a positive impact on the local ecosystem almost immediately.

453
00:35:48,941 --> 00:35:51,040
They trap sediment over time.

454
00:35:51,041 --> 00:35:54,180
Then you've got vegetation that comes in, starts filling in.

455
00:35:54,181 --> 00:35:59,180
You've got a wetland and you've got wetland plants, emergent vegetation, and that creates

456
00:35:59,181 --> 00:36:02,420
great habitat for a number of species.

457
00:36:02,421 --> 00:36:06,380
Big dams often simply submerge riparian habitats.

458
00:36:06,381 --> 00:36:12,100
lush life-sustaining areas beside rivers, beaver dams have the opposite effect.

459
00:36:12,101 --> 00:36:16,900
Some studies have shown something like seven to eight times as much riparian habitat as

460
00:36:16,901 --> 00:36:19,560
a result of the beaver dams coming in.

