00:38:33,587 --> 00:38:38,140
Roughly 550 or so dams have been removed in the United States already,

492
00:38:38,540 --> 00:38:45,320
over 200 since 1999. In the case of the Cascades Dam in Yosemite National Park,

493
00:38:45,321 --> 00:38:49,820
removal would mean returning a protected environment to a more wild state.

494
00:38:49,821 --> 00:38:54,620
I'm standing here on the banks of the Merced River here in Yosemite National Park.

495
00:38:54,621 --> 00:38:58,120
We're about three miles downstream for Yosemite Valley

496
00:38:58,121 --> 00:39:01,620
and right at the former site of the Cascades Diversion Dam.

497
00:39:01,621 --> 00:39:07,920
This dam was built in the early 1900s to generate hydroelectric power for Yosemite Valley.

498
00:39:07,921 --> 00:39:12,920
When the dam was taken offline in 1985 and no longer produced power,

499
00:39:12,921 --> 00:39:15,880
the dam and its structures were obsolete,

500
00:39:16,420 --> 00:39:19,900
so we at the National Park Service decided to remove the dam.

501
00:39:21,080 --> 00:39:26,320
Completed in 1917, the 184-foot-long timber crib dam

502
00:39:26,321 --> 00:39:29,880
was constructed of logs spiked together to form cribs

503
00:39:29,881 --> 00:39:31,400
that were filled with rocks.

504
00:39:32,300 --> 00:39:35,980
Sheathed with redwood boards and flanked by 30-foot-high concrete abutments,

505
00:39:36,520 --> 00:39:39,820
this rudimentary dam presented challenges to the planners of its removal.

506
00:39:39,821 --> 00:39:48,200
There was a huge concern that we would remove the dam and there would be this huge washing of sediments downstream.

507
00:39:49,120 --> 00:39:54,580
What it cost $200,000 to build would cost $2.8 million to demolish.

508
00:39:55,600 --> 00:40:00,260
After three years of planning, a crew of 30 working with a bulldozer and an excavator

509
00:40:00,261 --> 00:40:06,040
removed the dam in just two months, November and December of 2003.

510
00:40:06,041 --> 00:40:14,980
workers diverted the river to one side removed half the dam and then rerouted the flow back and

511
00:40:14,981 --> 00:40:20,500
demolished the other side of the dam engineers had tested sediment levels and found them to be low

512
00:40:20,501 --> 00:40:27,760
so sediment didn't wash down and choke the river as they had feared workers used excess sediment

513
00:40:27,761 --> 00:40:32,980
to fill the hole where the dam had been with the dam out of the way the environmental team

514
00:40:32,981 --> 00:40:35,860
set about restoring the habitat to that of a free-flowing river.

515
00:40:36,740 --> 00:40:38,820
We had a number of areas to look at.

516
00:40:38,920 --> 00:40:42,320
We had the channel itself that was going to be exposed.

517
00:40:42,860 --> 00:40:46,000
We had a floodplain, and then we had this bank.

518
00:40:46,360 --> 00:40:49,340
And each of them had their own unique issues.

519
00:40:50,140 --> 00:40:53,280
The channel itself is going to drop 12 feet.

520
00:40:53,640 --> 00:40:56,160
That means that the water table is going to drop 12 feet.

521
00:40:56,760 --> 00:41:00,680
The floodplain is going to expand, and that's a great opportunity for us

